    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p><strong>Is an under desk elliptical good exercise?</strong> It is good light activity at almost any pace, and it becomes real moderate exercise when you push the resistance and speed up enough to breathe harder. The difference matters, because only moderate minutes count toward the 150 minutes a week that health guidelines recommend.</p>
<p>Most people use their machine at an easy pace while typing. That is valuable for cutting sitting time, but it is not a workout in the fitness sense. The good news is that turning some of those minutes into moderate exercise is simple once you know how to check your effort.</p>
<p>This guide covers intensity, the talk test, what an under desk elliptical trains and what it misses, and a weekly plan that makes it part of a complete routine.</p>

<h2>Intensity: Light Activity vs Real Exercise</h2>
<p>Exercise scientists sort activities by intensity using METs (multiples of your resting energy use). Under 3.0 METs is light; 3.0 to 5.9 is moderate; 6.0 and above is vigorous.</p>
<p>Seated pedaling sits at around 2.0 METs at a light pace and about 2.8 at a moderate one. So, judged by intensity alone, is an under desk elliptical good exercise? Typical use falls in the light zone, just under the moderate threshold. With higher resistance and a faster cadence, many people can push past it for short or focused sessions, especially if they are less fit to begin with. For the full comparison with walking and a gym elliptical, see <a href="/how-effective-is-an-under-the-desk-elliptical/">how effective an under the desk elliptical is, in METs and calories</a>.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>How you use it</th><th>Intensity</th><th>Counts toward 150 minutes?</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Slow pedaling while typing</td><td>Light</td><td>No</td></tr>
<tr><td>Steady pace on a call, low resistance</td><td>Light</td><td>No</td></tr>
<tr><td>Higher resistance, faster pace, breathing harder</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hard intervals, can only say a few words</td><td>Vigorous (briefly)</td><td>Yes, and vigorous minutes count double</td></tr>
<tr><td>Motorized, passive mode</td><td>Light</td><td>No</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2>Does It Count Toward the CDC's 150 Minutes?</h2>
<p>The CDC and WHO recommend at least 150 minutes of moderate aerobic activity a week (or 75 minutes of vigorous), plus muscle-strengthening activity on two or more days. An under desk elliptical can contribute to the aerobic part, but only for the minutes you spend at moderate intensity or higher.</p>
<p>That means a day of easy pedaling while you work, however long, does not tick the box by itself. A 20-minute block at a brisk pace and higher resistance does.</p>
<h3>The talk test</h3>
<p>You do not need a heart rate monitor to tell the difference. Use the talk test, which the CDC recommends:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Light:</strong> you could sing comfortably. Your breathing barely changes.</li>
<li><strong>Moderate:</strong> you can talk in full sentences but cannot sing. Your breathing is noticeably quicker and you may start to sweat lightly after 10 minutes.</li>
<li><strong>Vigorous:</strong> you can only say a few words before needing a breath.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you are on a call and can chat normally without any change in your breathing, you are in the light zone. That is fine for the call; just do not count those minutes toward your 150.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> moderate effort is hard to combine with precise typing. Plan your moderate blocks for calls where you mostly listen, video training, reading or a dedicated break.</p></div>

<h2>Is a Seated Elliptical Good Exercise for Every Part of Fitness?</h2>
<p>Fitness has several components. An under desk elliptical is strong on some and absent on others, which is why it works best as one piece of a routine.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Fitness component</th><th>Does it train it?</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Cardiovascular endurance</td><td>Partly</td><td>Only during moderate or harder blocks.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Leg muscular endurance</td><td>Yes</td><td>Quads, hamstrings, glutes and calves work for long stretches.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Strength</td><td>Barely</td><td>Resistance is too light to build real strength.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Joint mobility (hips, knees, ankles)</td><td>Yes</td><td>Smooth, repeated, low-impact range of motion.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Upper body and core</td><td>No</td><td>Arms and torso stay still.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Balance</td><td>No</td><td>You are seated and supported.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bone loading</td><td>No</td><td>No impact or body weight on your legs.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>For a closer look at the muscles involved, read <a href="/what-muscles-does-an-under-desk-elliptical-work/">what muscles an under desk elliptical works</a>. To fill the gaps in balance and bone loading, walking is the obvious partner; our comparison of <a href="/is-under-desk-elliptical-as-good-as-walking/">under desk elliptical vs walking</a> explains how the two complement each other.</p>

<h2>Are Sitting Ellipticals Good for You Even at Light Intensity?</h2>
<p>Yes, for a different reason. Light activity does not build much fitness, but it cuts down uninterrupted sitting, which health guidelines now treat as a separate concern from exercise. Light pedaling raises your energy use above sitting, keeps your legs from stiffening and, according to many owners, helps them feel less sluggish in the afternoon.</p>
<p>So think of an under desk elliptical as doing two jobs: light activity that fills the hours you would otherwise sit still, and, when you push it, moderate exercise that counts toward your weekly target. For the broader research on sitting and NEAT, see <a href="/does-an-under-desk-elliptical-work/">does an under desk elliptical work</a>.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><p>If you have a heart condition, high blood pressure, joint problems or are recovering from surgery, check with your doctor before adding moderate or vigorous blocks.</p></div>

<h2>Is an Under Desk Elliptical Good Exercise for Beginners and Seniors?</h2>
<p>For people starting from a low fitness level, an under desk elliptical is often better exercise than it is for fit adults. Intensity is relative: a pace that feels easy to a regular runner can feel moderate to someone who has been inactive for years, or to an older adult. Run the talk test rather than assuming your sessions are light.</p>
<p>The seated position also removes the main barriers that stop beginners exercising: fear of falling, joint pain from impact, and the effort of getting to a gym. You can start with five-minute blocks at low resistance and build up week by week. Many seniors use one while watching TV, which makes daily practice easy.</p>
<p>Motorized models such as the [[amazon:ancheer|ANCHEER]] can help people who cannot pedal against resistance yet, as long as you treat passive mode as mobility work and gradually pedal along with the motor. Our <a href="/best-under-desk-elliptical-for-seniors/">best under desk elliptical for seniors</a> guide covers stable, easy-to-use options. As always, check with your doctor first if you have a health condition.</p>

<h2>Is Cubii Good Exercise?</h2>
<p>The Cubii is the best-known seated elliptical, so it comes up often. A Cubii is good low-impact leg exercise with quiet magnetic resistance and multiple levels, which matters here: the higher levels are what let you reach moderate effort. The [[amazon:cubii-jr2|Cubii JR2]] is the standard model, and the [[amazon:cubii-jr2-plus|Cubii JR2+]] adds Bluetooth tracking if you want to log your moderate minutes in an app.</p>
<p>The same rules apply to any brand: the machine is only as much exercise as the effort you put in.</p>

<h2>How to Turn Seated Pedaling Into a Real Workout</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Set up for power.</strong> Sit upright with your back against the chair and knees slightly below hip height at the top of each stride. A chair that does not roll, or [[amazon:caster-cups|caster cups]], lets you push harder without sliding away.</li>
<li><strong>Raise resistance first, then speed.</strong> Higher resistance at a steady cadence feels more like exercise and is easier to sustain than spinning fast against nothing.</li>
<li><strong>Use timed blocks.</strong> Aim for at least 10 minutes at moderate effort per block. Short, timed blocks make it easier to know when you are in the moderate zone and when you can ease off.</li>
<li><strong>Add intervals.</strong> Alternate 1 minute hard with 2 minutes easy for 15 minutes.</li>
<li><strong>Check with the talk test.</strong> Mid-block, try saying a full sentence out loud. If you could sing it, add resistance.</li>
</ol>
<p>Use the timer below to run your moderate blocks and interval sessions:</p>
